            <ArticleTitle>Headache and prolonged dilatation of the middle meningeal artery by PACAP38 in healthy volunteers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>140-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0333102411431333</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore a possible relationship between vasodilatation and delayed headache we examined the effect of pituitary adenylate cyclase-activating polypeptide-38 (PACAP38) on the middle meningeal artery (MMA) and middle cerebral artery (MCA) using high resolution magnetic resonance angiography (MRA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled study 14 healthy volunteers were scanned repeatedly after infusion (20 min) of 10 pmol/kg/min PACAP38 or placebo. In addition, four participants were scanned following subcutaneous sumatriptan (6 mg).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We found significant dilatation of the MMA (p = 0.00001), but not of the MCA (p = 0.50) after PACAP38. There was no change after placebo (p &gt; 0.40). Vasodilatation (range 16-23%) lasted more than 5 h. Sumatriptan selectively contracted the MMA by 12.3% (p = 0.043).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">PACAP38-induced headache is associated with prolonged dilatation of the MMA but not of the MCA. Sumatriptan relieves headache in parallel with contraction of the MMA but not of the MCA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
